(borge-bug (id "BORGE-1790546246")
  (title "notes outline write path: durability follow-ons after BORGE-1790535211 (upsert-in is two atomic ops not one; tree/delete has no before-image so history is not a safety net for deletes; the malformed-outline refusal is a droppable string)")
  (status triage)
  (created 2026-09-27)
  (filed-by roerick)
  (doc "The follow-ons recorded in BORGE-1790535211 (closed) that are NOT in the code or an open issue.

1. notes/doc/upsert-in is TWO atomic operations, not one.
   It writes the note row (tree/upsert path \"note\") and THEN calls
   notes/tree/save-outline. b4337b5 made save-outline itself atomic, so the
   destructive sequence is safe, but a failure BETWEEN the two steps leaves a
   NEW/updated note row with the OLD outline (title/tags/kind updated, headings
   untouched), and the caller gets an error. Fix: wrap upsert-in's body in one
   with-transaction (nested joins are safe). Same one-line treatment as the
   four functions already wrapped.

2. tree-time tombstones carry body=null by design.
   History is a before-image system for WRITES; a delete is recoverable only
   from the last non-tombstone gen of the SAME path. A rename/move followed by
   a delete, or a recursive subtree delete over paths that never carried their
   own live gen, are NOT recoverable from tree_versions. The rule now says
   \"do not rely on history for deletes\", which is honest but leaves no
   safety net. Options: (a) accept and keep the validation+ordering guard as
   the only defense; (b) give tree/delete an opt-in before-image capture;
   (c) make the recursive-delete path refuse when any descendant path lacks a
   live gen. Decide explicitly.

3. notes/doc/upsert-in's guard returns a value, not a raise.
   A malformed outline returns a \"refused: ...\" STRING, which a caller using
   (seq ...) can silently ignore (the AGENTS.md deny_anon comment calls out
   exactly this hazard for a different case: \"a returned string is silently
   dropped by (seq ...), so a multi-write handler could partially succeed past
   an anonymous denial\"). Current callers (notes/ui/do-save) check it, but the
   safe shape is to RAISE (or return a distinguishable error value) so a
   future caller cannot ignore the refusal.


   RECURRENCE LESSON (why BORGE-1786860090 did not prevent this).
   The 2026-08-16 incident was the SAME data loss with a DIFFERENT trigger:
   notes/tree/write-root-body threw `unbound: n` (a parallel `let` whose
   binding referenced a sibling) AFTER notes/tree/save-outline's
   clear-headings had already run, so any notes/doc/upsert on a note with
   kids emptied it — live cost then: the shared agents note, 93 nodes -> 0.
   Commit 8622096 fixed the TRIGGER (parallel let -> let*) and BORGE-1786860090
   described BOTH the trigger and the ordering (\"clear-headings DELETES all kid
   nodes first ... so kids are gone before the error\"), but the ordering itself
   was LEFT IN PLACE, so when the next trigger arrived (the alist body-boundary
   rejection in tree/upsert, BORGE-1790535211) the identical destruction
   recurred nine weeks later.
   LESSON: when a bug report contains a destructive STRUCTURE (delete-then-do,
   clear-then-rebuild) alongside its immediate trigger, fixing only the trigger
   leaves the bug live. The structure must be corrected or an issue explicitly
   left open for it. BORGE-1786860090 never stated an ordering follow-on, was
   never closed, and sat in triage while the same note was emptied a second
   time. save-outline is now write-then-sweep + atomic (b4337b5); this issue
   tracks what remains.
")
)
